WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:03.040
Film Courage: You left Los Angeles and you’re
back in Washington [state].

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:04.180
What was the impetus for this?

00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:06.029
Author and Creative Jeff Leisawitz: Moving
to Washington?

00:00:06.029 --> 00:00:07.029
Film Courage: Yes.

00:00:07.029 --> 00:00:09.690
Jeff Leisawitz: I just needed a new scene.

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:12.420
I need some more green.

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:17.099
I kind of like the rain, I mean I do like
rain.

00:00:17.099 --> 00:00:18.831
Film Courage: It’s beautiful up there.

00:00:18.831 --> 00:00:19.960
Jeff Leisawitz: Rain down on me.

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:24.769
Film Courage: There you go and you’re a
[The] Who fan.

00:00:24.769 --> 00:00:26.949
But at what point?

00:00:26.949 --> 00:00:29.460
Because everybody talks about going somewhere
else.

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:32.489
But a lot of people don’t do it.

00:00:32.489 --> 00:00:35.260
What was it where you just said “It’s
time.

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:36.260
NFA.

00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:38.710
I want to change my course in life.”

00:00:38.710 --> 00:00:44.750
Jeff Leisawitz: Well, one of the pieces was
I was young, I was like 24, 25.

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:53.190
So when we’re younger we have more flexibility
I think in our choices and in our lifestyles

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:54.350
and things like that.

00:00:54.350 --> 00:01:02.600
On of the things that I try to get across
to my college students that I teach song writing

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:05.979
and recording to college students which like…that’s
a job?

00:01:05.979 --> 00:01:06.979
Are you kidding me?

00:01:06.979 --> 00:01:10.660
I love it.

00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:17.640
But I try to get them to understand the power
and flexibility of their youth because they

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:22.940
don’t really know it because they haven’t
been 15 years older yet.

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:25.409
They haven’t been in the workforce and running
around.

00:01:25.409 --> 00:01:28.920
They are just getting out of school or in
school.

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:30.560
So that was a piece of it.

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:38.149
Being young and I knew sort of where I wanted
to go with my life but it just seemed like

00:01:38.149 --> 00:01:40.060
a good place.

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:44.530
Film Courage: Another thing that I say a lot
in these interviews but I’m going to say

00:01:44.530 --> 00:01:50.020
it again is that when you are 20 or 24 people
give you pats on the back and they give you

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:51.020
“Attagirl!

00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:52.020
Attaboy!

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:53.020
You can do it.”

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:55.439
When you are 40, they don’t do that.

00:01:55.439 --> 00:02:00.159
And I think that part of it is there is part
of them that wants to do that and they can’t

00:02:00.159 --> 00:02:04.090
[mostly from obligations] and it’s all very
real thing in the adult world.

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:10.539
You have certain roles and obligations and
so you don’t get as many Attagirls!

00:02:10.539 --> 00:02:11.539
[or Attaboys!].

00:02:11.539 --> 00:02:13.040
Jeff Leisawitz: That’s true.

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:16.190
Maybe you can’t drop everything and move
to different city.

00:02:16.190 --> 00:02:22.790
But you always can do something and that is
the key, right?

00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:26.959
A huge difference is you’ve got to pay the
rent and whatever the deal is.

00:02:26.959 --> 00:02:29.730
Okay, I get that, of course.

00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:36.540
But if you want to be a screenwriter, spend
a half an hour a day or two hours on a Saturday

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:37.629
or whatever it is.

00:02:37.629 --> 00:02:42.480
A little bit and you absolutely can do that.

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:45.010
It’s just about prioritizing, right?

00:02:45.010 --> 00:02:48.159
Are you watching TV or are you writing on
your screenplay?

00:02:48.159 --> 00:02:49.770
I mean…let’s get real.

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:53.330
Film Courage: Going back to the image in the
beginning of your book [NFA: The No B Guide

00:02:53.330 --> 00:02:54.330
For Getting Your Creative Dreams Off The Ground].

00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:58.400
Jeff Leisawitz: Are you going to the bar or
are you practicing guitar, right?

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:03.250
There’s a million ways to go, there’s
a million things that you probably don’t

00:03:03.250 --> 00:03:04.250
need in your life.

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:06.700
Do you have to play video games for two hours
a day….hhmmm?

00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:09.849
Probably not, you know.

00:03:09.849 --> 00:03:13.140
You can do something productive with that
if you choose.

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:17.510
Film Courage: What about that being the lonelier
way?

00:03:17.510 --> 00:03:24.129
Because going to the bar there are 20 other
people there that can be part of what…and

00:03:24.129 --> 00:03:26.090
this isn’t my quote ‘The Ain’t it Awful
Club.’

00:03:26.090 --> 00:03:28.290
It’s from author Peter McWilliams, he wrote
a book [You Can't Afford the Luxury of a Negative

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:29.290
Thought - The Life 101 Series].

00:03:29.290 --> 00:03:31.460
And he said there is one [The Club] in every
workplace, there’s one in every bar.

00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:32.460
And it’s great.

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:36.250
You can commiserate, we know politics is a
hot button right now.

00:03:36.250 --> 00:03:39.760
But you go back home and you write that screenplay,
it’s pretty much just you.

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:40.760
It’s lonely.

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:43.280
Jeff Leisawitz: It is lonely.

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:45.870
Life is lonely, right?

00:03:45.870 --> 00:03:49.940
But it’s yours to choose.

00:03:49.940 --> 00:03:55.409
So if you want to be in that club, go ahead.

00:03:55.409 --> 00:03:59.689
But don’t whine to me about it or yourself.

00:03:59.689 --> 00:04:06.959
Because we all have power and perhaps it’s
lonely writing by yourself but guess what?

00:04:06.959 --> 00:04:11.879
There’s a million writing clubs out there,
writing Meetups and things like that.

00:04:11.879 --> 00:04:13.849
So join one of those.

00:04:13.849 --> 00:04:17.380
And suddenly you’re all writing by yourself
but at least you’re at the same table.

00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:18.850
I mean I’ve done that a million times.

00:04:18.850 --> 00:04:22.170
Film Courage: Is that why you write in coffee
shops or maybe you don’t (I’m just assuming).

00:04:22.170 --> 00:04:25.290
Jeff Leisawitz: I do sometimes, sometimes
not.

00:04:25.290 --> 00:04:28.750
I mean that’s why half the people go to
coffee shops.

00:04:28.750 --> 00:04:35.150
In Seattle you will see 40 people in a room,
four or five of them are talking to each other

00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:38.510
and everybody else is on the computer doing
something.

00:04:38.510 --> 00:04:40.460
Why do they do that?

00:04:40.460 --> 00:04:44.320
Because they want to be together even though
they are separate.

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:48.341
And I mean I’m not saying it’s the greatest
thing in the world.

00:04:48.341 --> 00:04:53.010
If you’re together, you kind of want to
be together, but it’s something.

